  "readme": "## README.md\n# globcolour-Icechunks\n\nThis is an icechunk for [Copernicus globColour products](https://data.marine.copernicus.eu/product/OCEANCOLOUR_GLO_BGC_L4_MY_009_104/description). Note Copernicus provides a Zarr lazy load of its data with:\n\n```\nimport copernicusmarine\nds = copernicusmarine.open_dataset(\n    dataset_id=\"cmems_obs-oc_glo_bgc-plankton_my_l3-multi-4km_P1D\",\n)\n```\n\nThe icechunk provides an alternate access via a virtual store on Source Coop (AWS us-west-2). This will be faster in the US but slower, sometimes much much slower, in the EU and slower than using Copernicus' JupyterHub which is on CloudFerro in the same region as their data.\n\n```\nimport icechunk as ic # need icechunk > 2\nimport xarray as xr\n\nurl = \"https://data.source.coop/fish-pace/globcolour/cmems_obs-oc_glo_bgc-plankton_my_l3-multi-4km_P1D\"\nstorage = ic.http_storage(url)\nrepo = ic.Repository.open(storage)\nauth = {p: ic.credentials.HttpAccess for p in repo.config.virtual_chunk_containers or []}\nstore = repo.reopen(authorize_virtual_chunk_access=auth).readonly_session(\"main\").store\n\nds = xr.open_zarr(store, consolidated=False, chunks=None)\nds\n```\nUse `create_default_indexes=False` in `open_zarr()` to circumvent the high I/O cost associated with creating the coords. Doesn't seem to affect xarray much. This will be particularly noticeable when I/O latency is high, e.g. when outside of US. The icechunk is in AWS us-west-2.",
